sections.text:
Click a signal to see:

- **Where** it was found;
- **What changed**: before and after, with the periods compared;
- **Evidence and links**;
- **Related KPIs**;
- **Used by actions**: the actions built from it.

Copies of one finding, such as the same prompt text tracked as several prompts, are grouped into one row and counted once.

Open a task to see:

- **What**, **URL** (the exact page), **Where** and **Why**;
- **Change**: the text before and after, or the text to add;
- **How**: one step per line;
- **Done when** and **How we check**;
- **Type** (Content, On-page, Technical, Outreach, PR or Product), **Effort** and **Role**.

Click **Edit** to change any of them.

One or two sentences above the cards sum up the range. Each fact appears only when the data can back it. The cards:

- **AI-referred visits**, with the change on the previous period;
- **Top platform** and **Platforms**;
- **AI share of traffic**, **Engaged visits** and **Key events from AI**, which need Google Analytics;
- **Google clicks**, which needs Search Console. Google reports about two days late, so 24h and 7d show its latest reported days.

After you publish it, click **Check again**: Ansvisor reads your home page once and looks for the snippet. Your home page is also checked when the brand is set up, or once when you open this page if it never was and no AI visit has arrived: the card reads **Checking your home page…** meanwhile. The snippet itself adds no request for your other visitors.

The **Tracking snippet** card then reads:

- **Active · last visit …**: an AI-referred visit in the last week;
- **Installed · no AI visits in the last 7 days**: the check found it, or it has sent a visit before;
- **Not detected on** your domain: neither, so the page doesn't show 0 visits as a result.

Added the snippet with a tag manager? The check can't see it in the page, so the card changes with the first AI visit.

The **Gap list** view shows domains cited in at least two answers that name a competitor while your brand isn't named and none of your domains is cited, and never cited in an answer where you appear. It lists up to 100 domains, strongest first, with these columns:

- **Domain**;
- **Source type**;
- **Competitor answers**: how many of those answers cite it;
- **Which competitors**: the competitors those answers name;
- **Strength**.

**By competitor** shows one competitor at a time: pick it from the list to see the third-party domains cited in the answers that name it, with these columns:

- **Domain** and **Source type**;
- **Answers feeding**: how many answers that name the competitor cite the domain;
- **Also cites us?**: whether the domain also appears in an answer where you appear;
- **Strength**.

Open **Competitors** from the sidebar, or from **Brand Visibility**:

- **Head-to-Head** on the **Leaderboard** card;
- a competitor's name on the Leaderboard, which opens the page with that competitor selected;
- **Head-to-Head Comparison**, shown in the Leaderboard's place before a brand has competitors.

The Action Center's **Add at least 3 competitors** step in Getting started opens it too.

Only admins and managers add, suggest and remove competitors; other members see the list and the comparison.

- a suggested title;
- a content type: blog post, comparison page, landing page, FAQ page, guide or case study;
- a target word count;
- keywords;
- a call to action;
- an outline of three to eight sections, each with its key points;
- competitor insights: the competitors cited and their visibility advantage.

- the action to take, with what it was built for: a topic and its number of prompts, **Single prompt**, or the Action Center action it came from;
- when it was **Suggested**, with a dot on the rows suggested since your last visit;
- its **Type**: **Owned** or **Earned**, and, where it applies, what to do with your page: Create, Optimize, Expand, Refresh, Consolidate or Defend;
- its **Impact**, **High**, **Medium** or **Low**, which Ansvisor rates when it writes the opportunity, and its **Score**, from 0 to 100;
- its **Status**: New, Reviewed, Sent to Workflow, In Progress, Done, Dismissed or Archived.

Above the list, cards count the **Total Opportunities** and the **High Impact** ones, and give the **Avg. Score**. On plans with monthly limits, **This billing period** shows how many new opportunities and content briefs are left, and when they reset.

Each row's **…** menu has the quick actions:

- **Send to Editor**, or **Open draft** once a draft is being written for it;
- **Send to Plan**, or **Open in Plan** once it's a priority there, for owned opportunities still open;
- **Send to Workflow**, for a new opportunity;
- **Open in new tab**, which opens it as a page of its own;
- **Dismiss**, for a new one. Undo it from the message that appears.

Send to Plan and Dismiss are for admins and managers. Click an opportunity's title to open it in a panel beside the list, with all its data and the rest of its actions.

**Mark ready** opens **Before you publish** first. It checks:

- placeholders, alt text and links;
- the author;
- the qualified reviewer on pages that advise on health, money or the law;
- visible dates;
- the structured data to paste, if your CMS doesn't add it;
- a warning when Trust is under 60.

Copy and Export carry **Reviewed by** beside **Author** in the page metadata. Once the page's text names the reviewer, its structured data names them too, apart from the article author. On a page with a shopping path, **Before you publish** also names product cards whose price has changed in your catalog since they went in. **Send to CMS** opens it only when it has something to ask about.

The page goes as Export makes it, without its H1: your CMS's title field carries the title. Ansvisor holds the send while:

- a placeholder in square brackets is still open. **Fill now** selects the first one;
- the page has no title, or nothing under its title;
- the slug can't be used.

Pick **Create from scratch** to write a new page from:

- a **Content Opportunity**;
- a **Prompt**. Prompts where AI already names or cites you in the last 30 days aren't listed, as they need no new page;
- a **Topic** that has tracked prompts.

**Send to Editor** on an opportunity opens New content with the opportunity already picked: a new page, or the page of yours it names to optimize. Earned opportunities don't go to the editor.

On **Select your target market**, pick the region and language your audience uses. For the United States you can also pick a state, which localizes AI answers to it; leave it empty for nationwide results.

Your choice decides:

- the language your suggested topics and prompts are written in, and so the language AI engines answer in;
- the location your prompts are asked from;
- the competitors suggested in step 5.

You can track prompts in more locations later.

On **Review your prompts**, Ansvisor writes five starter prompts for each topic you chose: real questions a potential customer would ask an AI engine. Review the list:

- remove prompts that don't fit;
- remove a whole topic from its **…** menu with **Remove topic**;
- add your own prompts under each topic.

If the list holds more prompts than your plan allows, the step says how many to remove before you can continue. The prompts are asked daily, on every AI platform your plan allows.

Keep prompts free of your brand name. A question about your brand by name almost always scores high, because the AI talks about you when asked, so it doesn't measure how AI engines find you on their own.

Volumes come from an analysis you run:

- **Analyze Volumes** the first time;
- **Analyze New Prompts** for prompts added since, shown while some have no volume;
- **Refresh Volumes** for newer Google data;
- **Re-analyze Keywords** to analyze every prompt again, keywords included.

On plans with a monthly analysis limit, each analysis counts against it, and the toolbar shows how many are left. **Tracked Prompts** also warns when some prompts haven't been analyzed yet.

Open the same dialog in **Remove** mode from:

- the **…** menu on a location's column in the Locations view, then **Stop tracking in** that location;
- the **×** on a tracked cell (**Stop tracking here**), when the prompt is tracked somewhere else too;
- **Locations › Remove locations** in the bar for selected prompts;
- **Remove** at the top of **Track in more locations**.

Pick the locations and the prompts. A prompt needs at least one location, so one tracked only in the places you stop keeps them.

**By prompt**, the view the tab opens on, lists your tracked prompts with answers in the range. Each row shows:

- the engines that answered it, coloured when they shared searches for it and faded when they didn't;
- **Answers with fan-out**: how many of its answers ran a search at all;
- **Sub-queries**: how many searches they ran.

Expand a prompt to see its searches, each with its **Engine**, **Times searched** and **Intent**.

**High frequency** lists every search behind your prompts' answers, the most searched first:

- **Sub-query**: the search the engine ran;
- **Engine**: the engines that ran it;
- **Times searched**: how often it ran in the range;
- **Sourced prompts**: the tracked prompts whose answers ran it;
- **Intent**: the kind of search, such as How-to, Comparison or Best / Top.

Both views have a search box to narrow the list.

The **Topic leaderboard** has a row per topic:

- the topic's name and how many prompts it has;
- **Visibility**: the 0 to 100 score over the topic's answers, the same score as on **Brand Visibility**;
- **SoV**, **Mentions** and **Citations**;
- **Trend**: a small line and the change in points against the window of the same length just before;
- **Top competitor**: the competitor with the largest share of voice in the topic;
- **Last run**.

Click **Prompts**, **Visibility**, **SoV**, **Mentions**, **Citations** or **Last run** to sort by it; the table opens sorted by Visibility. Click a topic to open its page.

A topic's page shows all of its results, not only the date range picked on the Topics tab:

- **Visibility**, **Brand mentions**, **Citations** and **Positive sentiment** for the topic;
- **Visibility trend**: your daily visibility on the topic against the competitor average;
- **Share of voice by platform**: you against competitors on each answer engine;
- **Competitor comparison**: the top six brands on this topic, ranked by their score, with yours marked **YOU**;
- **Prompt performance**: the topic's **Top performers** and the prompts under **Needs improvement**, from its last 50 runs.

**View all prompt results for this topic** opens **Brand Visibility** filtered to the topic.

Each night the Action Center reads each page's latest audit from the last 90 days and turns failed signals into actions:

- **Fix low-scoring pages**, for audited pages scoring below 50;
- **Fix AI crawler accessibility**, **Fix structured data issues** and **Fix outdated content**;
- **Fix weak entity signals**, **Fix internal content connectivity** and **Fix content gaps**.

An action lists the pages it covers and the failed checks on each, with **Open Site Audit**.

The **Signals** section lists every check by category, each with a pass, warning, fail or n/a icon and its impact: **high**, **medium** or **standard**. **Issues only** hides the passing ones. Open a signal to see:

- the **Finding**: what this audit saw on the page, when the check reports it;
- what the signal measures, and **Why** it matters to AI engines;
- the **Recommended fix** written for this page, or the general **Fix** for signals without one.

- **Audit selected** on the **Opportunities** tab;
- **Re-audit** on the pages you select on the **Pages** tab;
- **Audit page** on a landing page of the **AI Referral Traffic** page, or **Audit pages** for several;
- the re-run icon at the top of a report, to audit the same page again;
- auto-audit, which audits your top-priority pages on its own.

Audits started in bulk run a few at a time.

The **Opportunities** tab is an inbox of pages worth auditing next. Each row shows a **Priority** from 0 to 100, the page, **Why** it's listed, its **AI visits** over the last 30 days and its **Last audit**. A page is listed when:

- **No AI traffic**: it ranks high on your site in Google Analytics but gets no visitors from AI engines. **Targeted, not cited** adds that a tracked prompt points at it and no AI answer cites it;
- a competitor is cited on prompts where you never are, and this is your closest page;
- it was **Never audited**, or not in the last 90 days.

AI visits need Google Analytics or the tracking snippet.

Each answer also gets its own score from 0 to 100, shown with that answer:

- up to 40 points for naming you, 10 per mention;
- up to 30 for citing you, 15 per citation;
- up to 15 for your share of the answer's citations;
- up to 15 for its tone: 15 when positive, 7 when neutral and you're named.

The head-to-head comparison on the **Competitors** page averages these per-answer scores.

The score reads only AI answers, so work that doesn't change them doesn't move it, for example:

- backlinks without on-page content;
- how often you post on social media, which AI engines usually weigh less than published content;
- traditional SEO work that doesn't address how AI engines pick content.

As a rough guide, not a promise, a focused program in a competitive category often gains 5 to 10% a month, relative to its score. Faster growth usually needs a major press placement or a structural content investment.

Once you know what moved the score, pick one of three:

- publish content aimed at the prompt;
- earn a placement on a site AI engines cite;
- accept it as a one-time dip.

A score may also dip after you add new prompts, until those prompts mature.
